</span></div>
</figure>
<div id="A2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#A2.T11" title="Table 11 ‣ Appendix Appendix B Evaluation ‣ AgentRaft: Automated Detection of Data Over-Exposure in LLM Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a> presents the manually labeled source and sink functions for each Agent, where source functions return or obtain user information, and sink functions transmit data to third-parties.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A2.F7" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2603.07557v1/Case_Study.png" id="A2.F7.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:452/282;" width="452" height="282" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 7. </span>Case study of real-world LLM Agent app (Shortwave <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">ove, 2026b</a>)</cite> and Pokee <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">ove, 2026a</a>)</cite>) with DOE.</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="A2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">Appendix B.1. </span>Case Study</h3>

<div id="A2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we show two real-world LLM Agent applications, <span id="A2.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Shortwave</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">ove, 2026b</a>)</cite> and <span id="A2.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Pokee</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">ove, 2026a</a>)</cite>, to demonstrate the practical impact of DOE risks. In the Shortwave scenario, a user instructs the agent to retrieve a specific “Workspace ID” from a setup email and share it with a collaborator via the <span id="A2.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">read_email</span> <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="A2.SS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">send_email</span> chain. However, the agent fails to enforce a strict data boundary and inadvertently leaks high-sensitivity “Integration Tokens” and “Bot Access Tokens” within the outgoing message. Similarly, in the Pokee case, a user asks the agent to read a spreadsheet containing “Personal Account &amp; Platform Binding” information and send the “Zoom Account ID” to an online course redemption provider. While the user’s intent is strictly limited to a single platform identifier, the agent’s <span id="A2.SS1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">search_file</span> <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="A2.SS1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">read_google_sheets_data</span> <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="A2.SS1.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">send_email</span> execution chain over-shares the entire record, exposing private contact details such as the user’s “Bound Mobile Phone” and “Bound Email”. Both cases illustrate a critical misalignment between user intent and agent execution, where the lack of fine-grained data filtering leads to the unauthorized exposure of private user assets in the current LLM Agent ecosystem.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</article>
